WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.367
If a CASE expression has no ELSE and none of the WHEN conditions match, PostgreSQL returns NULL.

00:00:06.367 --> 00:00:09.487
Sometimes that is exactly what you want.

00:00:09.487 --> 00:00:13.122
NULL might mean unclassified or not applicable.

00:00:13.122 --> 00:00:15.817
But other times it creates ambiguity.

00:00:15.817 --> 00:00:23.212
In this example, certain respiratory diagnoses receive a label and everything else falls through to NULL.

00:00:23.212 --> 00:00:27.837
When you look at the output, ask whether that NULL is meaningful.

00:00:27.837 --> 00:00:30.462
Does it mean “not respiratory”?

00:00:30.462 --> 00:00:33.497
Does it mean “unknown diagnosis”?

00:00:33.497 --> 00:00:36.547
Or does it mean we forgot to define a category?

00:00:36.547 --> 00:00:40.457
Imagine a dashboard where category is displayed to users.

00:00:40.457 --> 00:00:46.372
A blank value may look like missing data rather than an intentional “other” group.

00:00:46.372 --> 00:00:50.592
In that case, an ELSE 'Other' branch might be clearer.

00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:57.782
On the other hand, in a quality-control query, leaving unmatched values as NULL can be useful because it

00:00:57.782 --> 00:01:00.632
highlights records that need review.

00:01:00.632 --> 00:01:06.207
The important point is to decide rather than drift into the behavior accidentally.

00:01:06.207 --> 00:01:09.817
The same idea applies to missing input values.

00:01:09.817 --> 00:01:17.732
If a NULL bill should be labeled Needs Review, create an explicit WHEN bill_amount IS NULL branch.

00:01:17.732 --> 00:01:22.380
Do not rely on it falling through unless that is truly the intended meaning.
